WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.079 --> 00:00:01.968
- This is a very exciting moment for me.

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:04.763
My life is all shit, fires, and issues.

00:00:04.763 --> 00:00:08.930
So for Tyler to say this
is a good one, I'm excited.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.817
CEO of (bleep) or (bleep)?

00:00:15.962 --> 00:00:16.799
Amazing.

00:00:16.799 --> 00:00:17.634
(laughing)

00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:19.709
We'll be beeping two of the words in there

00:00:19.709 --> 00:00:21.360
but it went well, it was good.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.027
(hip-hop music)

00:00:28.066 --> 00:00:30.733
(phone ringing)

00:00:32.528 --> 00:00:35.183
So do you wanna focus on
that part of my career,

00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:36.709
like why I thought I was able to go from

00:00:36.709 --> 00:00:38.343
being a wine retailer into kind of

00:00:38.343 --> 00:00:41.473
this media thing I've become?

00:00:41.473 --> 00:00:44.801
When it comes to business
I've always been fearless.

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:47.422
I didn't start swimming until I was nine.

00:00:47.422 --> 00:00:51.615
I was scared of dogs
pretty much my whole life.

00:00:51.615 --> 00:00:53.831
I don't like heights all that much.

00:00:53.831 --> 00:00:56.291
I'm scared of snakes like,
I'm not trying to tackle them,

00:00:56.291 --> 00:00:58.064
I have no interest in
hanging around with snakes.

00:00:58.064 --> 00:01:00.337
I think everybody's got their
strengths and weaknesses.

00:01:00.337 --> 00:01:02.894
When it comes to business,

00:01:02.894 --> 00:01:05.734
boy have I been fearless my whole life.

00:01:05.734 --> 00:01:08.057
Sometimes people wait
until their parents die

00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:10.761
so they can run the business, you know?

00:01:10.761 --> 00:01:12.534
I went a different route.

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:15.228
I wanted to give back to my parents,

00:01:15.228 --> 00:01:17.651
I built the business for them

00:01:17.651 --> 00:01:21.624
and I then I checked that box
and took my skills elsewhere.

00:01:21.624 --> 00:01:22.914
I will say this, I think

00:01:22.914 --> 00:01:27.130
the surrounding neighborhoods
of Manhattan have a thing.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:29.919
When you're in the heart of it right,

00:01:29.919 --> 00:01:31.733
the heart of the world,
I mean New York City is

00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:33.958
literally, literally, maybe,

00:01:33.958 --> 00:01:37.216
arguably the most important
city in the world.

00:01:37.216 --> 00:01:39.163
You know when you're within miles of that,

00:01:39.163 --> 00:01:42.167
you feel that heartbeat but
you're still not it, right?

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:43.928
So you have a little bit of
that chip on your shoulder

00:01:43.928 --> 00:01:46.346
and there's a little bit
of that bridge and tunnels,

00:01:46.346 --> 00:01:49.164
a little bit of like,
you're the second tier

00:01:49.164 --> 00:01:51.425
of the ultimate A tier.

00:01:51.425 --> 00:01:53.191
I'll be honest with you, I think that is a

00:01:53.191 --> 00:01:55.283
disproportionate advantage.

00:01:55.283 --> 00:01:58.460
I think it's the most
similar thing to actually

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.545
being an immigrant in America.

00:02:01.545 --> 00:02:04.108
You know, it's having that
little bit of hunger and chip

00:02:04.108 --> 00:02:06.971
and then if you're lucky
enough to be an immigrant

00:02:06.971 --> 00:02:09.534
and grow up in Jersey,
I think it's an edge.

00:02:09.534 --> 00:02:13.398
I think it's an intensity,
a vibe, a comet,

00:02:13.398 --> 00:02:16.981
a level of common sense,
it's an awareness.

00:02:18.032 --> 00:02:21.599
Yeah, I think it's a really,
pretty special thing.

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:24.022
Embrace the chip on your shoulder

00:02:24.022 --> 00:02:28.024
and expand on it and triple down on it

00:02:28.024 --> 00:02:31.282
and really use it to your advantage.

00:02:31.282 --> 00:02:35.135
Awesome, you're welcome,
take care, bye-bye.

00:02:35.135 --> 00:02:36.254
- I have one more question

00:02:36.254 --> 00:02:37.528
that I've been wondering.

00:02:37.528 --> 00:02:39.693
Who would you say that
besides I guess family members

00:02:39.693 --> 00:02:41.886
has been your biggest role model?

00:02:41.886 --> 00:02:42.719
- Nobody.

00:02:42.719 --> 00:02:43.552
- Nobody?

00:02:44.544 --> 00:02:46.249
- That's the truth.

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:47.349
- No one's ever like

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:49.567
inspired you to do something?

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:53.024
- I'm sure, but I can't figure it out.

00:02:53.024 --> 00:02:53.857
The real answer--

00:02:53.857 --> 00:02:54.975
- 'Cause you're trying to figure it out

00:02:54.975 --> 00:02:56.997
or something you've just
kind of, have accepted?

00:02:56.997 --> 00:02:58.747
- Like do you know who, you know who,

00:02:58.747 --> 00:03:00.567
because they're not,
it's not like I've ever

00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.338
spent any thoughtful time in saying

00:03:02.338 --> 00:03:06.184
Richard Branson did this or
I've just never done that.

00:03:06.184 --> 00:03:09.849
Like kindness and compassion
and empathy and gratitude

00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:13.561
and good guys and girls inspire me,

00:03:13.561 --> 00:03:17.982
like oh that's a nice
person, that's a good dude,

00:03:17.982 --> 00:03:20.815
that gets me going and then the market.

00:03:20.815 --> 00:03:22.065
Like everybody.

00:03:23.011 --> 00:03:24.232
Like the game.

00:03:24.232 --> 00:03:27.381
The game, the market, the process.

00:03:27.381 --> 00:03:30.048
But I've never really, you know?

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:33.174
- You seen that video
of Matthew McConaughey,

00:03:33.174 --> 00:03:35.499
like my role model is like me in 10 years?

00:03:35.499 --> 00:03:36.726
Like that's kind of--

00:03:36.726 --> 00:03:38.845
- That's interesting, I
kinda, that's interesting.

00:03:38.845 --> 00:03:40.519
- You try to be yourself in 10 years

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:41.884
and when you get there,
obviously you're not--

00:03:41.884 --> 00:03:43.135
- That's cool.

00:03:43.135 --> 00:03:46.122
That's a better articulation probably.

00:03:46.122 --> 00:03:47.742
- Yeah, that's how I kind of--

00:03:47.742 --> 00:03:51.075
- I'm very insular, like really insular.

00:03:52.294 --> 00:03:55.710
Like really, like I don't
even know about people.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:56.543
You know?

00:03:57.384 --> 00:03:59.695
Like the thought to
take time to do homework

00:03:59.695 --> 00:04:02.763
on somebody that I admire

00:04:02.763 --> 00:04:05.634
is like going to outer space.

00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:07.668
- You also have to do the
homework, like you said

00:04:07.668 --> 00:04:09.085
building relationships,
you have to kind of

00:04:09.085 --> 00:04:10.551
know the people that you're meeting.

00:04:10.551 --> 00:04:13.317
- Yeah but I treat, notice
it's a good microcosm

00:04:13.317 --> 00:04:14.846
back to the (bleep) thing.

00:04:14.846 --> 00:04:18.175
You're right but I'm agnostic.

00:04:18.175 --> 00:04:20.383
I'm basically talking
to you as hard as I'd be

00:04:20.383 --> 00:04:23.419
talking to my next meeting
which is Wladimir Klitschko.

00:04:23.419 --> 00:04:25.822
Which is true, literally
Wladimir Klitschko

00:04:25.822 --> 00:04:27.938
is walking in right now
but I'm not gonna try

00:04:27.938 --> 00:04:30.521
any harder, it's just the same.

00:04:31.555 --> 00:04:32.694
- I respect that.

00:04:32.694 --> 00:04:36.116
- Yeah it ended up being a
great attribute in hindsight.

00:04:36.116 --> 00:04:38.759
- And you find that people respect that?

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:39.664
Like they don't--

00:04:39.664 --> 00:04:43.173
- I think people subconsciously feel it.

00:04:43.173 --> 00:04:46.699
I think big name people
realize I don't give a fuck

00:04:46.699 --> 00:04:51.647
and I think people on the
coming up appreciate it.

00:04:51.647 --> 00:04:53.064
- That makes sense.

00:04:53.064 --> 00:04:54.651
It's like they've been treated

00:04:54.651 --> 00:04:56.074
